WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.480
This node graph represents a legacy engineering instinct and react native development,

2
00:00:05.480 --> 00:00:10.120
dumping all application data into a single unified global store.

3
00:00:10.120 --> 00:00:13.440
This is the coupling of two incompatible data types,

4
00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:17.960
a synchronous remote server data and synchronous local UI data.

5
00:00:17.960 --> 00:00:21.400
This coupling creates immediate architectural symptoms.

6
00:00:21.400 --> 00:00:25.800
The first is the necessity of writing manual cache and validation logic,

7
00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:30.520
forcing developers to manage the timing of remote data refreshes by hand.

8
00:00:30.520 --> 00:00:32.840
The second symptom follows directly.

9
00:00:32.840 --> 00:00:36.920
Because the local store inevitably falls out of sync with the remote source,

10
00:00:36.920 --> 00:00:40.280
stale data bugs begin appearing within the user interface.

11
00:00:40.280 --> 00:00:43.520
The third symptom is the emergence of race conditions.

12
00:00:43.520 --> 00:00:47.440
Multiple asynchronous fetches conflict within the single store,

13
00:00:47.440 --> 00:00:50.480
leaving the user interface in a non-deterministic state

14
00:00:50.480 --> 00:00:52.600
that is unresponsive to the user.

15
00:00:52.640 --> 00:00:56.840
These are not inherent limitations of the react native framework.

16
00:00:56.840 --> 00:00:59.760
They are the direct result of an architectural assumption

17
00:00:59.760 --> 00:01:02.160
regarding how state should be managed.

18
00:01:02.160 --> 00:01:05.760
Predictable scaling requires strict state bifurcation.

19
00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:08.800
We must isolate data into distinct categories

20
00:01:08.800 --> 00:01:11.920
and apply specialized tools to govern each domain.

21
00:01:11.920 --> 00:01:15.440
Strict state decoupling provides the structural isolation needed

22
00:01:15.440 --> 00:01:17.520
to remove manual caching logic,

23
00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:21.520
providing a pathway to enterprise scale stability in react native.

24
00:01:21.560 --> 00:01:24.480
This split-screen diagram illustrates the solution.

25
00:01:24.480 --> 00:01:28.480
On the left is server state, data originating on a remote database

26
00:01:28.480 --> 00:01:32.080
that must be continuously fetched, cached, and synchronized.

27
00:01:32.080 --> 00:01:33.920
The industry standard enterprise tool

28
00:01:33.920 --> 00:01:37.320
to manage this asynchronous domain is TANstack Query.

29
00:01:37.320 --> 00:01:39.440
On the right side, we have client state.

30
00:01:39.440 --> 00:01:43.440
This is ephemeral data existing only during the application's lifecycle,

31
00:01:43.440 --> 00:01:47.280
dark mode preferences, local cart items, or active off-sessions.

32
00:01:47.280 --> 00:01:51.040
The tool best suited for managing this local synchronous domain

33
00:01:51.040 --> 00:01:52.360
is Zustant.

34
00:01:52.360 --> 00:01:54.360
The primary architectural constraint

35
00:01:54.360 --> 00:01:58.880
is that these two state domains must never be mixed into the same store.

36
00:01:58.880 --> 00:02:01.320
Violating this firewall forces developers

37
00:02:01.320 --> 00:02:04.120
to manually write distributed systems logic,

38
00:02:04.120 --> 00:02:09.160
like network retries and caching, inside a simple UI state manager.

39
00:02:09.160 --> 00:02:13.400
Respecting this boundary offloads complex caching to TANstack Query,

40
00:02:13.400 --> 00:02:15.920
reducing the application's total code base

41
00:02:15.920 --> 00:02:19.080
and lowering the cognitive load on the engineering team.

42
00:02:19.080 --> 00:02:21.720
Looking specifically at the client state domain,

43
00:02:21.720 --> 00:02:24.280
local UI state can still become a bottleneck

44
00:02:24.280 --> 00:02:26.800
as an applications feature set expands.

45
00:02:26.800 --> 00:02:28.680
This block diagram shows what happens

46
00:02:28.680 --> 00:02:32.360
when a single sprawling Zustant store grows unchecked.

47
00:02:32.360 --> 00:02:34.880
It becomes unmanageable for large teams,

48
00:02:34.880 --> 00:02:36.280
generating merge conflicts

49
00:02:36.280 --> 00:02:39.200
and triggering unnecessary global re-renders.

50
00:02:39.200 --> 00:02:41.560
The standard scaling solution for this bottleneck

51
00:02:41.560 --> 00:02:43.560
is the Zustant Slices pattern.

52
00:02:43.560 --> 00:02:48.600
This pattern organizes the state into modular domain-specific architectures.

53
00:02:48.600 --> 00:02:51.720
One slice exclusively for user authentication,

54
00:02:51.720 --> 00:02:54.160
another isolated for shopping cart logic,

55
00:02:54.160 --> 00:02:57.240
bringing development into native mobile alignment.

56
00:02:57.240 --> 00:02:59.560
The conceptual purpose of a Zustant Slices

57
00:02:59.560 --> 00:03:03.040
maps directly to the role of an Android view model class.

58
00:03:03.040 --> 00:03:05.480
By trading slices like native view models,

59
00:03:05.480 --> 00:03:08.280
engineers enforce a separation of concerns,

60
00:03:08.280 --> 00:03:10.720
where a minor UI update in the shopping cart

61
00:03:10.720 --> 00:03:14.200
cannot trigger a cascading re-render in the authentication flow.

62
00:03:14.200 --> 00:03:16.760
Switching focus back to the server state domain,

63
00:03:16.760 --> 00:03:18.640
simple fetch calls are insufficient

64
00:03:18.640 --> 00:03:21.640
for an enterprise hand stack query implementation.

65
00:03:21.640 --> 00:03:24.120
A common vulnerability in standard caching logic

66
00:03:24.120 --> 00:03:26.360
is the reliance on hard-coded string keys

67
00:03:26.360 --> 00:03:28.480
to identify and retrieve data.

68
00:03:28.480 --> 00:03:32.360
Simple string typos inevitably bypass cache and validation rules.

69
00:03:32.360 --> 00:03:34.120
This creates silent data failures

70
00:03:34.120 --> 00:03:36.720
that are difficult to track down during debugging.

71
00:03:36.720 --> 00:03:39.160
This visual code flow representation

72
00:03:39.160 --> 00:03:41.600
demonstrates the structural solution,

73
00:03:41.600 --> 00:03:44.000
typed query key factories.

74
00:03:44.040 --> 00:03:47.440
A typed factory ensures absolute query consistency

75
00:03:47.440 --> 00:03:51.680
and type safety across large multi-developer code bases.

76
00:03:51.680 --> 00:03:55.000
Once robust type safety is securely established,

77
00:03:55.000 --> 00:03:57.440
architecture must account for data latency

78
00:03:57.440 --> 00:04:00.120
through proactive background data fetching.

79
00:04:00.120 --> 00:04:02.760
This introduces the second major pattern,

80
00:04:02.760 --> 00:04:05.200
prefetching in tan stack query.

81
00:04:05.200 --> 00:04:07.880
This diagram shows a background data pipeline

82
00:04:07.880 --> 00:04:11.480
that closely mirrors the mechanics of Android's work manager.

83
00:04:11.480 --> 00:04:13.360
Using the work manager mental model

84
00:04:13.360 --> 00:04:15.560
helps developers structure their applications

85
00:04:15.560 --> 00:04:17.400
to ready data in the background.

86
00:04:17.400 --> 00:04:18.640
Ensuring it is available,

87
00:04:18.640 --> 00:04:21.680
the moment a user navigates to a new screen.

88
00:04:21.680 --> 00:04:23.880
The final hurdle in application latency

89
00:04:23.880 --> 00:04:25.720
occurs during mutations,

90
00:04:25.720 --> 00:04:28.960
waiting for server confirmation when a user alters data,

91
00:04:28.960 --> 00:04:31.000
such as pressing a like button.

92
00:04:31.000 --> 00:04:34.040
The primary method for reducing this perceived network lag

93
00:04:34.040 --> 00:04:37.680
is the third core pattern, optimistic updates.

94
00:04:37.680 --> 00:04:41.320
This sequential flow diagram maps out the process.

95
00:04:41.320 --> 00:04:45.280
You instantly mutate the local UI state upon user action,

96
00:04:45.280 --> 00:04:46.800
operating under the assumption

97
00:04:46.800 --> 00:04:49.480
that the server request will eventually succeed.

98
00:04:49.480 --> 00:04:51.920
The safety net here is that tan stack query

99
00:04:51.920 --> 00:04:55.160
automatically rolls back the UI to its previous state

100
00:04:55.160 --> 00:04:58.080
if the background server request ultimately fails.

101
00:04:58.080 --> 00:05:01.800
Combining key factories, prefetching and optimistic updates

102
00:05:01.800 --> 00:05:03.520
transforms tan stack query

103
00:05:03.520 --> 00:05:07.240
into a resilient offline capable synchronization engine.

104
00:05:07.240 --> 00:05:09.320
Returning to our split screen diagram,

105
00:05:09.320 --> 00:05:11.200
we can now view the fully realized

106
00:05:11.200 --> 00:05:14.520
react native dual engine architecture in operation.

107
00:05:14.520 --> 00:05:18.000
Zoo stan slices handle the immediate local UI logic,

108
00:05:18.000 --> 00:05:21.600
while tan stack query strictly governs remote synchronization.

109
00:05:21.600 --> 00:05:23.440
Enforcing this strict separation

110
00:05:23.440 --> 00:05:26.120
addresses the structural causes of loading state bugs

111
00:05:26.120 --> 00:05:28.400
and replaces the single store monolith

112
00:05:28.400 --> 00:05:30.680
with a predictable modular system.

113
00:05:30.680 --> 00:05:34.240
However, even perfectly architected state engines

114
00:05:34.240 --> 00:05:37.240
will occasionally encounter unpredictable network

115
00:05:37.240 --> 00:05:39.600
or logic failures in production.

116
00:05:39.600 --> 00:05:43.480
This introduces the necessity of structured observability.

117
00:05:43.480 --> 00:05:45.640
When the state drops, the engineering team

118
00:05:45.640 --> 00:05:47.920
must know exactly why.

119
00:05:47.920 --> 00:05:51.480
This sets up the next critical phase of enterprise scaling,

120
00:05:51.480 --> 00:05:54.560
implementing century, structuring crash reporting,

121
00:05:54.560 --> 00:05:56.760
and building global error handling patterns

122
00:05:56.760 --> 00:05:59.760
to catch the failures that state engines cannot prevent.

